{
  "note": "Shared by the Python server and the JS client. Single source of truth for all gameplay numbers. Distances in meters, times in milliseconds. Damage model: full `dmg` up to `rangeM`, then linear down to `dmg * falloff` at `falloffMaxM`, flat after that. A weapon with `lethalBody` one-shot-kills the torso between `lethalMinM` and `lethalMaxM`. Reload model: magazine guns use `reloadMs` (tactical) and `reloadEmptyMs` (from empty); clip-fed bolt-actions use `clip` instead - `fullMs` when the chamber holds 0-1 rounds, else `(clipMax - mag) * perRoundMs`, fed round by round so firing early keeps whatever is already in. Effects (`fx`) drive client presentation and aim recoil, not damage - the server never reads them, and the client falls back to documented defaults if one is missing, so a hand-edited file can never break a match. Per weapon: `flash` = muzzle flash size (1 = 0.30m), colour, muzzle light strength and duration; `kick` = viewmodel recoil `vm`, aim rise `punch` and sideways/visual `roll` in degrees; `tracer` = colour, `width` (x the 1.2cm base), lifetime and `every` (1 = every round); `smoke` = puff size, count and lifetime; `casing` = ejected brass size; `sound` = gunfire trim (`gain`, `rate` = playback pitch, `lpf` Hz low-pass, `tone` = synth fallback pitch), all relative to 1.0. Every display field carries its English twin (nameEn/descEn/roleEn/taglineEn/abilityNameEn/abilityDescEn) so the client can switch language without a second copy of these numbers.",
